WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.050
You are watching a video.

00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:03.050
Right now.

00:00:03.050 --> 00:00:04.050
This is a video.

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.960
You’re probably watching on your TV at home,
on your computer at the office, or on your

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.650
phone while sitting on the toilet.

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.000
While you watch, every second, there are millions
of pixels lighting up your screen with high-definition

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.730
images with rich stereo sound, both coded
with billions of bits streaming to you through

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:21.880
the internet.

00:00:21.880 --> 00:00:26.580
Mobile devices, smart TVs, and easy access
to high-speed internet have made this all

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.020
seem common.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.039
Trivial, even.

00:00:29.039 --> 00:00:34.300
But the magic of how these images and sounds
are coming to you, instantly, just about anywhere,

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.010
is a pretty incredible feat of engineering.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:41.730
The technology has become so commonplace that
the appearance of a buffering symbol, even

00:00:41.730 --> 00:00:46.129
on mobile internet, is considered a failure
of the streaming service.

00:00:46.129 --> 00:00:51.140
A problem we had to contend with while building
our own streaming service Nebula.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:57.079
And, as Wendover Productions did before me
in telling the story of why Nebula exists,

00:00:57.079 --> 00:01:02.180
I’m telling the story of HOW it exists,
and because it’s something I helped build,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.880
I am speaking directly to camera for the first
time.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:10.420
When we started out, Nebula didn’t have
a dedicated staff of engineers — it was

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:15.780
an experimental side-project — so we built
the first version on Zype, which offers white-label

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.979
streaming video services.

00:01:17.979 --> 00:01:22.980
Using their tools, we had a full, worldwide
service up and running in just a few months.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:27.460
The problem was that Zype wasn’t built to
handle the complexities of running hundreds

00:01:27.460 --> 00:01:32.610
of channels, or releasing hundreds of videos
per month, or streaming to hundreds of thousands

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.649
of active subscribers.

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:39.159
As Nebula outgrew them, we knew we needed
to build something fully custom, from the

00:01:39.159 --> 00:01:40.159
ground up.

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:43.070
A streaming system we named “Starlight.”

00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:47.270
From the software that compresses and delivers
the video to servers around the world, to

00:01:47.270 --> 00:01:52.470
the apps and browsers you watch them on, and
the internet in between, this technology has

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:55.440
advanced drastically in the last decade.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.320
This is how it works.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.619
If you were born after the year 2000, streaming
video might not seem like a big deal, but

00:02:05.619 --> 00:02:11.000
in the early Internet days of the 1990s, streaming
a video was basically unthinkable.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:16.200
In 1999, the trailer for Star Wars Episode
1: The Phantom Menace broke records by being

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.260
downloaded over one million times in 24 hours.

00:02:20.260 --> 00:02:26.019
Downloaded, not streamed, because streaming
a movie trailer at any level of quality simply

00:02:26.019 --> 00:02:30.440
wasn’t possible for the dial-up modem connection
speeds common at the time.

00:02:30.440 --> 00:02:35.610
In those days, most people connected to the
Internet using their telephone lines.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:40.099
Incredibly slow internet that was measured
in the kilobits per second, not the mega and

00:02:40.099 --> 00:02:42.879
giga bits we are accustomed to today.

00:02:42.879 --> 00:02:48.040
Those modems worked through modulation of
a digital signal onto the analog sound signal

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:51.209
being transferred over the copper telephone
wires.

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:56.209
If the amplitude of the sound was high, it
was a binary 1, if the amplitude was low,

00:02:56.209 --> 00:02:57.510
it was a 0.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:03:02.370
The transmitting computer would modulate the
data into sound waves, and the receiving computer

00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:03.590
would demodulate.

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.450
Modulate, demodulate.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:06.450
“Modem.”

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.000
To an extent, this is how most physical cables
work, short of fiber optics.

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:16.010
But doing this over copper telephone lines
is prone to loss, so the fastest speed you

00:03:16.010 --> 00:03:20.120
could possibly hope to get in the best of
conditions was 56 kilobits per second.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:27.370
For comparison, even a 720p YouTube video
has a bitrate of 5 megabits per second.

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:30.830
Around 90 times faster than a dial-up modem
could receive.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:36.230
By 2005, broadband connections like cable
modems were much more common, providing the

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:40.500
infrastructure for three guys in northern
California to start a video dating service

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:42.140
called “YouTube.”

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:46.620
That year, when Justin Timberlake and Janet
Jackson had their infamous “wardrobe malfunction”

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:51.260
at the Super Bowl halftime show, the trio
realized they couldn’t find any video of

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:56.970
the incident online, and repurposed YouTube
to be a digital video repository that anyone

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.170
could use.

00:03:58.170 --> 00:04:04.019
Nearly two decades later, home Internet speeds
have advanced significantly, but more crucially,

00:04:04.019 --> 00:04:08.849
mobile Internet connections have gotten so
fast that they often rival dedicated home

00:04:08.849 --> 00:04:09.849
connections.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:15.060
The smartphone revolution has driven cellular
data speeds to insane new levels, adding more

00:04:15.060 --> 00:04:19.310
and more frequencies and ways to squeeze more
data onto them.

00:04:19.310 --> 00:04:24.230
We now have the technologies to send the data
over long distances quickly, but that’s

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.900
just part one of the problem.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:31.009
When you clicked on this video you expected
to play immediately, without buffering.

00:04:31.009 --> 00:04:35.470
That can happen if the video is stored on
a server near you and you are one of the privileged

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:40.430
people to have access to fast internet, but
that isn’t always the case, and solving

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:46.160
those edge cases is where smaller indie streamers
like Nebula need to play it smart.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.710
The primary constraint is bandwidth.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:53.180
While high-speed connections are common in
the western world, not all users will have

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.150
fast internet access, and even if they do
have fast access network, problems can still

00:04:57.150 --> 00:04:59.970
occur which may lead to degraded performance.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:04.870
A poorly-shielded microwave can interfere
with Wi-Fi, Internet service providers may

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:09.479
have temporary slowdowns during times of peak
usage, and going through a subway tunnel on

00:05:09.479 --> 00:05:13.490
your morning commute may cause you to intermittently
lose signal

00:05:13.490 --> 00:05:18.280
To help combat these inconsistencies, the
server will encode the video several times

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.289
with different bitrates.

00:05:20.289 --> 00:05:24.289
Bitrate simply means the speed at which the
video will need to be transmitted in order

00:05:24.289 --> 00:05:26.889
to avoid buffering or pauses.

00:05:26.889 --> 00:05:31.350
Once the transcoder limits the bitrate, it
will also need to lower the resolution of

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:35.490
the video to keep the overall visual quality
of the video watchable.

00:05:35.490 --> 00:05:40.930
Low bitrates combined with high resolution
result in blocky video that is very distracting.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.889
The transcoder creates files with different
bitrates and resolution pairings, producing

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:50.460
low-quality smaller files all the way up through
larger 4K files.

00:05:50.460 --> 00:05:55.169
When a video player is streaming a video,
it isn’t downloading the entire file at

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:57.580
once, it downloads chunks at a time.

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:01.069
This allows for changes in streaming quality
during playback.

00:06:01.069 --> 00:06:05.110
If your internet connection slows down, the
service can switch to the lower-resolution

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:07.569
version of the file on-the -fly.

00:06:07.569 --> 00:06:12.020
The video may not look as good, but it’ll
keep playing, and hopefully switch back later

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.840
when the connection improves.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.349
It also allows for starting playback in the
middle of a video; if you want to pick up

00:06:18.349 --> 00:06:23.430
a video where you left off yesterday, there’s
no need to download the entire file.

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:29.509
Plus, since most videos don’t get watched
to 100% completion, this approach also saves

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:32.139
countless dollars on bandwidth bills.

00:06:32.139 --> 00:06:37.729
For Nebula’s “Starlight” system, the
team uses fragmented MP4s for this packaging.

00:06:37.729 --> 00:06:41.120
This lets us separate the audio files from
the video.

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.370
Audio requires much smaller file sizes compared
to video, and studies have shown that poor

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:52.270
audio quality will impact the viewer’s experience
more than poor video quality In other words,

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:55.240
a bad-sounding video will look worse.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:07:01.229
By separating audio and video, the team ensures
that the audio quality remains the same between

00:07:01.229 --> 00:07:02.870
all resolutions.

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:07.699
Once the files are split, text manifests are
written to describe the precise timing and

00:07:07.699 --> 00:07:10.710
file location for every chunk of video.

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:14.889
These manifests are what the video players
read when they load a video

00:07:14.889 --> 00:07:19.400
Once the central servers have finished processing
the new files, it needs to store them across

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:23.360
multiple different servers to ensure there
is a copy near the viewer.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:28.830
There’s a popular misconception that storage
and bandwidth are the expensive parts of running

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.370
a streaming service.

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:37.080
While file storage was once very expensive,
modern services like Amazon Web Services S3

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.639
and Backblaze B2 provide pretty inexpensive
storage.

00:07:41.639 --> 00:07:46.770
Around 3.7 million new videos are uploaded
to YouTube every day, so we imagine their

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:51.570
storage cost is higher than ours, but they
also own all of their data centers.

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:56.460
We think it’s safe to say that YouTube probably
pays less per video for storage than Nebula

00:07:56.460 --> 00:08:02.270
does, but for context, Nebula’s entire catalog
is currently around 30 terabytes.

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:05.650
We pay less than $200 USD per month to store
it.

00:08:05.650 --> 00:08:09.629
That’s less than I pay for my production
team's adobe subscriptions.

00:08:09.629 --> 00:08:15.300
With the files transcoded, packaged, and stored,
the next step is to deliver them to viewers.

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:19.759
When a user requests to watch a video, the
service could serve it to them directly from

00:08:19.759 --> 00:08:25.979
storage, but depending on where in the world
the viewer lives, that might be a slow process.

00:08:25.979 --> 00:08:27.979
This is why we need a CDN.

00:08:27.979 --> 00:08:31.360
CDN stands for content delivery network.

00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:35.979
This is a network of servers all over the
world, spaced out specifically to ensure that

00:08:35.979 --> 00:08:39.370
there’s a server near any given video viewer.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:44.390
When you tap to watch a video, the service
matches you to the CDN server closest to you

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:46.310
and starts the stream.

00:08:46.310 --> 00:08:51.160
The previous steps were all just preparation
to ensure that this local server had what

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:52.320
it needed.

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:58.310
CDNs have the capacity to transfer the 2,000+
terabytes of video data Nebula delivers every

00:08:58.310 --> 00:08:59.380
month.

00:08:59.380 --> 00:09:06.899
The CDN Nebula uses, Fastly, uses a 2-step
caching system utilizing three sets of servers.

00:09:06.899 --> 00:09:10.000
First there’s the local servers, called
“nodes”.

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:12.880
These are ideally located very close to you.

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.510
When your app or browser requests a video,
the service first checks for the files at

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:18.970
your local node.

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:22.390
If it doesn’t find them, it checks the caching
servers.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:26.380
The caching servers act as a second layer
of temporary storage.

00:09:26.380 --> 00:09:31.070
They contain all of Nebula’s catalog of
videos, allowing them to serve nodes in their

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:32.230
region quickly.

00:09:32.230 --> 00:09:37.769
Finally, there’s the Origin — the data
center with the servers and hard drives containing

00:09:37.769 --> 00:09:40.990
all of the original transcoded videos.

00:09:40.990 --> 00:09:46.829
Duplicating every video file on every node
around the world would take up a lot of space

00:09:46.829 --> 00:09:52.530
and use a lot of bandwidth, so the system
prioritizes newer, high-demand videos.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:57.690
If a video is especially popular, there’s
a good chance that someone in your geographical

00:09:57.690 --> 00:10:00.680
region has beaten you to watching it first.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:05.540
If the video is older and less popular, the
chances that it has that initial buffering

00:10:05.540 --> 00:10:06.990
time is higher.

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:10.050
It’s a lot like how retail supply chain
works.

00:10:10.050 --> 00:10:15.980
Items are produced at the factory (The Origin),
shipped to a warehouse (the caching servers),

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:21.020
and then distributed to local stores (the
nodes) for quick, convenient access.

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:25.980
If you experience stuttering or lag or general
poor video performance, this is often the

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.260
root cause.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:31.640
The video files are served to the CDN nodes
on-demand.

00:10:31.640 --> 00:10:36.050
If you’re using a service with millions
or billions of users, you’ll likely never

00:10:36.050 --> 00:10:39.769
be the first person to want to watch a particular
video.

00:10:39.769 --> 00:10:44.940
For a smaller service like Nebula, if you
live in an area where there aren’t many

00:10:44.940 --> 00:10:49.959
subscribers, you’re more likely to end up
waiting for that first transfer more often.

00:10:49.959 --> 00:10:55.490
As Nebula grows, this becomes, counter-intuitively,
less of a problem.

00:10:55.490 --> 00:10:57.490
Nebula is growing incredibly quickly.

00:10:57.490 --> 00:11:01.230
We have more than doubled our direct subscribers
in 2023.

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:06.730
I mentioned earlier that there’s a misconception
about storage and bandwidth being the expensive

00:11:06.730 --> 00:11:07.730
parts.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:12.029
We could only guess what someone like YouTube
pays for bandwidth and delivery for their

00:11:12.029 --> 00:11:19.639
videos, but for comparison, as of September
of 2023 Nebula currently has around 300,000

00:11:19.639 --> 00:11:26.760
active monthly users, and spends less than
$20,000 per month on bandwidth to serve all

00:11:26.760 --> 00:11:27.760
of them.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:32.139
The biggest hard cost for a streaming service
is hiring talented engineers.

00:11:32.139 --> 00:11:37.850
Building this complex system requires frontend
and backend web engineers to create the API

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:42.709
and build the website, development operations
people to make sure the systems are all healthy

00:11:42.709 --> 00:11:48.400
and running correctly, quality assurance people
to help find bugs in the code before it ships,

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:53.650
and countless product designers and app developers
to bring the experience to all of the devices

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:56.149
you want to watch videos on.

00:11:56.149 --> 00:12:01.459
To get a video playing on your screen, you
need an app with a video player.

00:12:01.459 --> 00:12:06.089
Sometimes this happens in a web browser, and
sometimes it happens in a dedicated app.

00:12:06.089 --> 00:12:10.470
For most Internet-enabled systems there’s
a client and a server.

00:12:10.470 --> 00:12:15.490
The client is the program making the requests,
and the server is the system that answers

00:12:15.490 --> 00:12:17.079
those requests.

00:12:17.079 --> 00:12:21.240
Most modern software is written so that the
majority of the logic and processing happens

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:23.839
on the server side, not in the client.

00:12:23.839 --> 00:12:26.480
This helps to simplify development across
devices.

00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:31.350
If there are going to be any major, showstopper
software bugs, it’s better to have them

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:35.949
happen on the server, where the engineers
and development operations teams can quickly

00:12:35.949 --> 00:12:42.190
roll out fixes without needing to go through
an app approval process or release new updates.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:46.910
For a video service, the server sends a list
of videos and all of the thumbnails to go

00:12:46.910 --> 00:12:52.019
with it, and the client app renders those
into some kind of human-readable list for

00:12:52.019 --> 00:12:53.080
you to browse through.

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.339
There are different kinds of apps.

00:12:55.339 --> 00:12:59.070
Web apps, mobile apps, desktop apps, TV apps.

00:12:59.070 --> 00:13:03.850
Different devices will often require that
apps be written in completely separate programming

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:08.160
languages —
like Swift for iOS, Kotlin for Android, or

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:13.730
JavaScript and TypeScript for smart TV, and
even incredibly niche languages like Brightscript

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:18.149
for Roku,
which makes hiring even more difficult.

00:13:18.149 --> 00:13:23.329
To confuse matters further, many of these
devices will require that apps be distributed

00:13:23.329 --> 00:13:28.230
through proprietary app stores with their
own approval processes.

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:33.560
When Nebula launched the LG Smart TV app earlier
this year, it took months to get it approved

00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:34.820
on the store.

00:13:34.820 --> 00:13:41.260
And, quick update, while we were working on
this video, LG told us that Nebula's LGBTQ

00:13:41.260 --> 00:13:48.199
content was a violation of their content policy
and the app needed to be removed in 46 countries.

00:13:48.199 --> 00:13:53.820
We asked for clarity on this policy, but were
only told that it was for "religious and cultural

00:13:53.820 --> 00:13:55.300
elements" in those countries.

00:13:55.300 --> 00:14:00.010
We're not really sure why they didn't remove
it themselves, and they haven't responded

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.480
to our requests for further discussion.

00:14:02.480 --> 00:14:07.930
Regardless of the outcome, having these conversations
and jumping through these hoops consumes a

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:12.640
lot of resources and is generally just a pain
in the ass.

00:14:12.640 --> 00:14:15.890
This is just one of the many apps we had to
develop.

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:21.050
Getting approval for iPhone updates, however,
typically happens in a matter of hours.

00:14:21.050 --> 00:14:26.800
Catering to each user's watching habits was
an expensive start up cost in development.

00:14:26.800 --> 00:14:32.529
While Nebula has only recently started tracking
watch time by device, nearly half of all YouTube

00:14:32.529 --> 00:14:34.680
views happen on TV screens.

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:39.420
If any streaming service wants to hold your
attention, it needs to be available no matter

00:14:39.420 --> 00:14:44.680
how you want to watch, and that means supporting
a wide variety of options.

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:49.250
Native mobile apps enable you to download
videos to watch offline, in case you’re

00:14:49.250 --> 00:14:51.560
on a long flight without Wi-Fi.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:55.050
TV apps make for comfortable viewing in your
living room.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:15:00.260
Web apps mean access from any device with
a web browser, which can have some surprising

00:15:00.260 --> 00:15:01.620
results.

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:06.320
Nebula even has a small number of viewers
who watch from the web browser in their Teslas.

00:15:06.320 --> 00:15:11.310
It can be tricky to account for all of the
variables in this equation.

00:15:11.310 --> 00:15:15.370
Different devices running different operating
systems running apps written in different

00:15:15.370 --> 00:15:20.649
languages, with different screen sizes and
design requirements, communicating over connections

00:15:20.649 --> 00:15:25.810
that could range from high-speed fiber optics
to a spotty cellular signal in an airport

00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:26.960
bathroom.

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:31.180
When you open your phone to watch a video,
what you’re really launching is a massively

00:15:31.180 --> 00:15:37.790
complex system that depends on a team of incredibly
skilled software engineers and designers who

00:15:37.790 --> 00:15:43.820
not only make all of this possible across
all of these scenarios, but make it look easy.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:48.300
Nebula is built as a partnership between the
platform and the creators.

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:53.269
The company provides the business structure,
design, and engineering that make up the platform,

00:15:53.269 --> 00:15:57.019
and the creators bring their videos and creative
vision.

00:15:57.019 --> 00:16:01.630
The platform will never be complete — that’s
the nature of software — but this partnership

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:05.060
has already led to a stable ecosystem.

00:16:05.060 --> 00:16:10.649
Nebula has hundreds of thousands of subscribers,
and the number two subscriber retention stats

00:16:10.649 --> 00:16:15.569
of any streaming service, second only to Netflix.

00:16:15.569 --> 00:16:20.199
This retention is possible because our audience
really cares about the creators and believes

00:16:20.199 --> 00:16:21.950
in what we're building.

00:16:21.950 --> 00:16:27.589
We respond by making increasingly better videos
for them to watch, which gives them a reason

00:16:27.589 --> 00:16:28.589
to stay.

00:16:28.589 --> 00:16:29.589
It’s a virtuous cycle.

00:16:29.589 --> 00:16:35.000
It's a very different kind of audience relationship,
and we want to safeguard that relationship

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:40.260
and ensure Nebula stays true to the people
who supported it from day one.

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:44.560
That’s difficult to do when you take investment
from venture capital firms.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:49.100
We don’t want to do that and that limits
us in some ways.

00:16:49.100 --> 00:16:54.190
For a subscription business, the key is to
find a balance between cash and revenue.

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:56.019
Cash is money you have today.

00:16:56.019 --> 00:16:58.290
Revenue is money that comes in over time.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:03.550
If we were a VC-funded startup, we'd have
a bunch of cash and we'd spend it on things

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:07.130
we hope would bring in more revenue later,
like funding originals.

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:09.780
But we aren’t VC backed.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:11.370
We are fan supported.

00:17:11.370 --> 00:17:16.319
So instead of sacrificing the platforms autonomy
to shadowy figures in Silicon Valley.

00:17:16.319 --> 00:17:19.130
We turned to our most loyal subscribers.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:23.579
The truth is that we have 680,000 customers.

00:17:23.579 --> 00:17:28.890
We can see from our retention stats that many
of them have no intention to cancel their

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:29.890
subscription.

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:33.200
So, why not offer them a way to purchase a
lifetime subscription?

00:17:33.200 --> 00:17:39.400
If every single one of those subscribers purchased
a lifetime subscription for say, 300 dollars,

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:45.110
we would have 204 million dollars in the bank
to fund the projects they signed up to watch.

00:17:45.110 --> 00:17:49.990
So, that’s what we did, and it has allowed
us to raise capital without turning to venture

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:51.490
capital firms.

00:17:51.490 --> 00:17:54.630
You can buy a lifetime subscription right
now.

00:17:54.630 --> 00:17:57.710
That program was incredibly successful for
us.

00:17:57.710 --> 00:18:02.950
It turns out a lot of people don’t like
having recurring subscriptions every month

00:18:02.950 --> 00:18:07.289
and would prefer to just pay once upfront
for lifetime access.

00:18:07.289 --> 00:18:11.520
And we have used that funding to greenlight
a lot of really exciting projects.

00:18:11.520 --> 00:18:15.049
Including some from our team, that we can’t
yet announce.

00:18:15.049 --> 00:18:17.460
That’s unheard of for a streaming platform.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.740
It’s blurring the lines between Hollywood
and creator owned businesses.

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:25.500
We have even greenlit entire movies that our
creators have written, like Patrick Willems

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:26.990
and Abigail Thorne.

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:29.250
That’s ultimately our goal.

00:18:29.250 --> 00:18:33.960
To give our creators opportunities that they
traditionally can't get, because some guy

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:37.049
in LA won’t even let them in the room.

00:18:37.049 --> 00:18:41.630
This is where the line between educational
video and ad becomes blurry, so I am going

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:43.360
to demarcate it.

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:46.740
This channel depends on the funding Nebula
provides us.

00:18:46.740 --> 00:18:50.679
If you have been subscribed to this channel
for more than 3 years, you have seen the huge

00:18:50.679 --> 00:18:54.450
increases in production quality that Nebula
has facilitated.

00:18:54.450 --> 00:19:00.250
We first started using 3D animations in the
logistics of d-day nebula original series.

00:19:00.250 --> 00:19:06.030
Those were all done within after effects,
which is extremely limited in 3D animation.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:12.070
Then we upgraded to blender and cinema 4D,
thanks to increased revenue driven by Nebula

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:13.070
sponsorships.

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:18.130
Now I think it's fair to say that we are a
world class engineering animation studio.

00:19:18.130 --> 00:19:21.559
Just look at our recent Space Shuttle series
for proof of that.

00:19:21.559 --> 00:19:24.130
We are committed to continuing that trend.

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:27.570
Investing more and more into our production
quality.

00:19:27.570 --> 00:19:32.940
Because I won’t be happy until we are THE
best engineering film studio on the internet.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:38.580
So please consider signing up for Nebula,
or even better switching to a lifetime membership.

00:19:38.580 --> 00:19:39.580
In the run up to the holidays we are also
offering gifted lifetime subscriptions.

00:19:39.580 --> 00:19:40.580
So you can gift Nebula to a friend or loved
one this Christmas.

00:19:40.580 --> 00:19:43.700
Of course, the regular monthly and yearly
options are still available too.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:49.140
You can sign up to a monthly, yearly or lifetime
membership with the link in the description

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:50.860
or sign up by pressing the button on screen
now.

